# Every loop in the unit graph, beside the product of the conversion factors round it. One row per
# loop, `violates` where one does not lie inside that product.
#
# A loop of conversions must come back
#   The rule: convert a quantity all the way round a loop of units and you must be able to get
#   back what you started with. `units/conversions.sqlc` reads each fusion part that carries a
#   factor as an edge from its part layer's unit to its composed layer's unit, so the filings make
#   a graph, and this walks it.
#
# Inside, not equal
#   One must lie inside the product rather than equal it, for two separate reasons.
#
# The factors are ranges
#   `merge-holding-composition` files its month as 672, 720 and 744 hours and says why: a month is
#   not 720 hours, and which one you got depends on which month. Multiplying ranges gives a range,
#   so the most a receiver can ask is that one is inside it.
#
# No document could close it exactly, even at the most likely value
#   Every loop in this graph runs through 720, and one over 720 has no exact decimal, so no pair of
#   factors a document can file multiplies to exactly one over 720. That comes from the corpus's
#   own calendar, not from anybody's care.
#
# The width comes from one month counted twice
#   It is not measurement error. The month that turns cards into card-hours is the same month that
#   turns node-hours back into cards. Multiplying the ranges treats them as two separate months and
#   spans nine per cent either way; a receiver who knew they were one month would get exactly one.
#   `merge-holding-composition/compute`'s shares and the composed remainder beside them show the
#   same thing. A tighter rule would accuse a correct filing, which is why this one is as loose as
#   it is.
#
# One row per loop, not per starting point
#   A three-edge loop is found three times, once from each unit. The walk keeps the one that starts
#   at the smallest unit, so a reader counts loops rather than starting points.
#
# No depth limit
#   A depth limit beside the path guard would be wrong, and composition/descent.sqlc says why: a
#   depth limit is a guess about how deep a legitimate composition goes. It would also add
#   nothing: the guard already refuses to revisit a unit other than the start, so the walk ends on
#   its own, after at most as many steps as there are units.
#
# What a limit would add is a blind spot
#   The number of units already limits any path without a loop, and a loop longer than an explicit
#   limit would be missed with no verdict saying the rule examined nothing and a clean pass. That
#   is worse than examining nothing: coverage would still show a row examined and passing.
#
# Probe
#   Put `WHERE w.depth < 6` beside the path guard, then raise it to 20, then tighten it below the
#   graph's own depth. The verdict must be identical in every case and to the unlimited walk; a
#   limit here removes nothing.
-- units/conversions.sqlc walked until a unit repeats; the product against one.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    WITH RECURSIVE e AS (
        :compose(units/conversions.sqlc)
    ),
    walk(start, at, depth, p_low, p_mode, p_high, path, filing, layer) AS (
        SELECT from_unit, to_unit, 1, factor_low, factor_mode, factor_high,
               ARRAY[from_unit, to_unit], filing, layer
        FROM e
        UNION ALL
        SELECT w.start, e.to_unit, w.depth + 1,
               w.p_low * e.factor_low, w.p_mode * e.factor_mode, w.p_high * e.factor_high,
               w.path || e.to_unit, w.filing, w.layer
        FROM walk w
        JOIN e ON e.from_unit = w.at
        WHERE NOT (e.to_unit = ANY (w.path[2:array_length(w.path, 1)]))
    )
    SELECT w.filing, w.layer,
           NOT (w.p_low <= 1 AND w.p_high >= 1) AS violates,
           format('%s: the factors multiply to [%s, %s] round it, and one %s inside',
                  array_to_string(w.path, ' to '),
                  round(w.p_low, 6), round(w.p_high, 6),
                  CASE WHEN w.p_low <= 1 AND w.p_high >= 1 THEN 'lies' ELSE 'does not lie' END)
           AS detail
    FROM walk w
    WHERE w.at = w.start
      AND w.start = (SELECT min(u) FROM unnest(w.path) u)
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'conversion_cycle_does_not_close'
